<%# The lab layout.

    It renders the ENGINE's real head partial, not a hand-rolled one. That matters
    more than it looks: layouts/studio/_head installs the Alpine `theme` store the
    navbar's toggle reads, publishes --nav-h/--nav-bottom, and sets
    `body { overflow-anchor: none }` — which the paint spec depends on, because
    scroll anchoring would otherwise drag scrollY when a bar's height changes and
    contaminate the very measurement that spec exists to take. A hand-rolled head
    would have quietly dropped all three, and the lane would have been grading a
    page no consumer ever renders.

    The asset tags at the end of that partial are the HOST's job. The dummy does
    them in E2eLabController::AssetDelivery, pointing at the engine's real
    app/assets files that e2e/boot.rb copies into public/e2e. %>
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en" class="dark">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
    <title>studio-engine e2e lab</title>
    <%= render "layouts/studio/head" %>
  </head>
  <body class="bg-page text-body">
    <%= yield %>
  </body>
</html>
